  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nThe core idea is to combine two optimization methods: gradient-based optimization for fine-tuning solutions and stochastic search for global exploration. Unlike the previous SLSQP-only approach, this algorithm maintains a population of diverse candidates and uses them to inform all optimization steps. The method works by dynamically adjusting the representation length of candidates during optimization, allowing the algorithm to efficiently explore solutions with varying complexities and avoiding premature convergence to local optima. We balance exploration and exploitation by using a fitness-probability scheduling mechanism and a multi-stage grid refinement strategy.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\nThe goal is to minimize:\n   C5 = max_k \u222b h(x) (1-h(x+k)) dx\nSubject to:\n   sum(h) = n/2\n   0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 for all i\n\nOur optimization metric keeps the same finite shift model. When evaluating candidates, we define:\n   f(h) = max_k (np.correlate(h,1-h,mode='full') * (2/n)) \n\nThe constrained representation is handled through two methods:\n   A. For gradient-based steps: use Lagrange multipliers to project normalized vectors back to the feasible set\n   B. For stochastic steps: apply component-wise clipping after random mutations\n\n3. Implementation procedure\nOur implementation executes in stages:\n\nPreinitialization:\n   - Load initial_h_values if provided, otherwise generate a random uniform vector\n   - Project the initial candidate to the feasible set via frobenius projection:\n        h0_clip = np.clip(initial_h_values, 0, 1)\n        normalized_sum = h0_clip.sum()\n        if abs(normalized_sum - 0.5*len(initial_h_values)) > 1e-3:\n            h0_clip += (0.5*len(initial_h_values) - normalized_sum) / len(initial_h_values) \n\nWith a balanced mix of gradient and stochastic operations:\n\nclass Population:\n    def __init__(self, size, grid_base):\n        self.grid_base = grid_base\n        self.current_size_\u03bc = 15   # Population size at the core\n        self.current_size_N = self.current_size_\u03bc * 10  # Initial density\n        self.h = self.generate_initial()\n        self.time_per_burn = 7.0   # Time allocated for each operation\n\n    def generate_initial(self):\n        h = (np.random.rand(self.current_size_N) - 0.5) * 2.0   # Starting from a uniformish baseline\n        h = np.clip(h, 0, 1)\n        # For projection that maintains integral constraint:\n        s = h.sum() - 0.5 * len(h)\n        # Only adjust if significant:\n        if abs(s) > 0.5:\n            s = s / len(h)\n            mask = h <= 0.95\n            h[mask] += s\n        return h\n\n    def optimize(self, max_budget_seconds):\n        start_time = time.time()\n        elapsed = 0\n        underlying_representation = self.grid_base  # 180-base grid\n\n        while elapsed < max_budget_seconds:\n            # Simulate proper multi-stage approach here...\n            for idx in np.random.permutation(len(self.h)):\n                elapsed += self.time_per_burn\n            \n            # More operations go here with structured timescattered updates\n\n        best_fitness = float('inf')\n        best solution = None\n\n        return best solution, best_fitness\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\n   - Default competition ratio (mutation vs fitness): 0.7 bias for least owned\n   - Representation scaling: automatic doubling at 200/400/600s milestones\n   - Acceptance threshold: 0.03 tolerance window at bottom and 0.05 at top\n   - Phase adaptation: \n        * Persistent burn-in phase (200s, 300 generations)\n        * Exploration burst phase (with representation increases)\n        * Convergence phase (narrowing focus)\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\n   Grid doubling at 200s: \n        * First doubling: n \u2192 2*n\n        * Second doubling: 2*n \u2192 4*n\n        * Final candidate is rendered at 4*n_points\n\n   Cooling schedule:\n        1. Initial high mutation rate of 0.25\n        2. Reduce by 20% per representation doubling\n        3. Final phase mutation rate below 0.05\n\n   Fallback mechanisms:\n        * Maintain historical backups (every 10 steps)\n        * If time exhausted, choose the best available candidate\n        * Ignore invalid candidate constraints during evolution\n\n6. Validation and return contract\n   Upon completion: \n        - Validate integral: require |sum(h) - 0.5*n_points| < 1e-6\n        - Check bounds: min(h) \u2265 0, max(h) \u2264 1\n        - Re-evaluate worst-value component\n        - Return h_final, c5_bound, n_points\n\nSanity checks during development but not in final execution:\n        - Ensure projection operations are O(n) complexity\n        - Verify cost model matches input grid doubling\n        - Self-time test on grid doubling codepath",
